WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.750
Film Courage: What are you top ten tips for
producing a movie?

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:08.220
John Paul Rice, Producer of A CHILD’S VOICE:
Top ten tips, wow.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:14.340
Script, passion about the script,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:23.089
Attack everything you do with reckless abandon
in terms of this is your passion,

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:28.599
Work with people that you get along with (you
have a mutual respect for) both in your profession

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:34.280
as well as there is a personal identification.
You don’t have to agree with everything

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:43.030
but a shared principle about how to work,
how you want to work (that three right?).

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:49.469
Don’t rush things. Don’t make artificial
deadlines that don’t mean anything other

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:55.210
than what you think if your head. That means
life for example don’t rush to get a film

00:00:55.210 --> 00:01:04.360
done cutting corners whether it be in preproduction,
production or post production all to meet

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.160
whatever deadline you’ve set that you have
to hit in order…if it’s a distributor

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:16.080
giving you a deadline, okay. If it’s you
trying to get in this one festival, you’ve

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:23.270
got to really weigh whether to cut those corners.
And the reason why I say that is in that haste

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:29.340
to get there you’ll forget certain things
that are important. And it’s not about just

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.510
racing to the deadline, it’s about thinking
your plan through very clearly so it ties

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:44.910
into that fourth and fifth part which is start
from the end and work your way back. Don’t

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:51.090
start here and go forward without knowing
where you’re going to end up and seeing

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:56.222
what that place looks like at the very end
when you arrive at the finished part of your

00:01:56.222 --> 00:02:03.170
film or the distribution of your film. It’s
like an objective (a clear objective) this

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:09.390
is where I need to be at this stage and it
should be a logical conclusion because everything

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:17.980
should flow forward as it flows backwards
as well. You won’t miss anything that way.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:24.890
Review your budget, look at it daily…all
the time. Even if you’re not a guy who writes

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:30.450
up the budget or a woman who writes up the
budget. Whatever it is, study it, become a

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:37.810
student of it because here is what will happen
and I know this from my own experience, we

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:43.840
had a problem on the last film, a piece of
equipment got broken and would have stopped

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:51.290
the whole filming but knowing what I had in
the budget, knowing what we had available,

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:57.310
I knew we could get through it and I was able
to make a split-second decision to start calling

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:02.760
and getting while I was in Riverside I had
to go to LA and I knew it was&nbsp;5:00&nbsp;o’clock

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.880
and everything closed at&nbsp;6:00 p.m.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.680
So I had to put game-mode on, I wasn’t gong
to get over there so I had to go “Who do

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.590
we know in LA that we can have once we purchase
it and go pick it up and I can go pick it

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:17.350
up later that night so I can drive it back
to set?”

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:25.300
But all of that I did over calm when what
had just unfolded could have been a potential

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:30.130
disaster but because I knew how much money
we had, I knew what we wanted to do, I knew

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:37.050
where to start, and within 30 minutes we had
an answer and a solution and it was done.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:43.270
So study the budget, it will benefit you in
ways that you won’t even know at the time.

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:46.880
Film Courage: That’s six?

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.792
John: We’re at six. I’m sure there are
more.

00:03:51.792 --> 00:03:58.090
Film Courage: Four more to go. I think the
most important thing is to have trust in the

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:05.670
people that you work with and that is incumbent
on you. In other words, you set the table

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:13.069
and everybody else that you invite to that
table is going to be participating in it.

00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:17.840
So vet the people you’re going to work with.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.610
For the actors (and this is just if you’re
doing the micro budget, you don’t have lawyers,

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:30.400
people to control the behavior of others),
don’t necessarily go for the most talented

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.500
if it’s going to cause you a headache. If
you know it’s going to cause you a headache

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:38.630
and it raises red flags, I meet with all the
talent one-on-one, after the audition, after

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:42.360
the call back, after the final callback. Some
of them don’t like that process that’s

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:47.680
fine. But the fourth and final is the meeting
one-on-one and I have to know where their

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.610
motivations are, what kind of person they
are, not as an actor but as the kind of person

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.080
they are going to be as a person on set&nbsp;
that I’m going to be in close quarters with,

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:02.319
I’m going to be working with four hours
long at a time. So just think and if you don’t

00:05:02.319 --> 00:05:09.240
find who you want and it doesn’t sit…it
doesn’t agree with you in here, keep looking,

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:15.020
keep looking to the very end. Go to every
avenue that you can. I did this with out leads.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:21.159
We had tons of talented actors and through
the process it just whittled itself down and

00:05:21.159 --> 00:05:25.039
the opportunity presented itself because I
didn’t give up and I didn’t stop looking

00:05:25.039 --> 00:05:34.379
and I found people that I just loved from
their profession to their person and I would

00:05:34.379 --> 00:05:40.110
work with them again in a heartbeat. If I
have a role, I’ll do it. They are just great

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:47.639
human beings. And they’re wonderfully talented.
So…we’re at seven? Eight, nine and ten?

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:52.569
Film Courage: I think you just have two more?

00:05:52.569 --> 00:06:05.400
John: Gosh, allow yourself to evolve with
the work that you’re doing. In other words

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:12.909
what you thought once when you started and
what happens there in on out, let that be

00:06:12.909 --> 00:06:20.150
your journey that guides you, in other words
let that part of the process enrich you and

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:25.889
give you that valuable feedback that you want
and need as a human being in order to grow

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:33.879
as an artist and in order to grow as a filmmaker.
I can’t say enough about this because I

00:06:33.879 --> 00:06:40.080
think it’s often that we ignore these things
that we’re human beings first and that we

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:49.620
have certain sensitives and things that bother
us and issues that come up and if our goal

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:57.499
and our focus is about the work and the relationship
to it in both ourselves and the people that

00:06:57.499 --> 00:07:03.619
we’re bringing to that table, you can achieve
so much more. I personally believe this just

00:07:03.619 --> 00:07:09.449
having the experience of doing it people will
work that little extra harder that you always

00:07:09.449 --> 00:07:14.539
wanted them to do without asking because you’re
leading by example and you’ve brought people

00:07:14.539 --> 00:07:22.409
into that mix that have the same, same characteristic
within themselves and it’s all the sudden

00:07:22.409 --> 00:07:27.259
“Oh? This person is like this. This person
is like this? Oh? I’m among people like

00:07:27.259 --> 00:07:32.249
me.” They’re all doing the same thing
where you have a sound guy who’s like taking

00:07:32.249 --> 00:07:39.439
bags out of things that are not his. Being
there to help you. This is what makes it worthwhile.

00:07:39.439 --> 00:07:43.330
It’s not struggle and all the hardships
and everything else that you have to deal

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:50.449
with. That’s fine but to override and overcome
all of that and deal with it head on, make

00:07:50.449 --> 00:07:56.779
it come from within you and let that idea
and inspiration and that passion flow and

00:07:56.779 --> 00:08:02.159
have other people come into it who feel that
passion in you and are inspired by it. It’s

00:08:02.159 --> 00:08:06.479
like “This is why I got into filmmaking.
I got into it for art and I’m hoping the

00:08:06.479 --> 00:08:09.099
art will give me the opportunity will give
me the opportunity for more art or bigger

00:08:09.099 --> 00:08:12.220
art or whatever it is.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:24.099
But that’s probably the most important thing
I can say is that allow yourself to learn

00:08:24.099 --> 00:08:32.099
from the process and don’t be so dogged
and fixated on a specific vision that it’s

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:38.539
not about being contrarian or throwing out
what you believe but let that belief system

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:43.529
evolve. Let it grow deeper, let it get richer,
let it enrich you and the people that you’re

00:08:43.529 --> 00:08:47.519
working from because at the end of the day,
at the end of all of this when I’m an old

00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:54.310
man regardless of where I end up, I have all
of these beautiful memories of working with

00:08:54.310 --> 00:09:02.459
people that and remembering those moments
on set and how I felt and that is like getting

00:09:02.459 --> 00:09:08.180
to relive your life a second time and enjoy
it. And I know that may be nine?

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:14.160
Film Courage: That’s okay, it’s two-in-one
right there. That was so excellent. I think

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:19.130
that may be why with your last film (I know
you have two more in pre-production) A CHILD’S

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:23.980
VOICE, you felt like even at that premiere
and you’re basically closing down [staying

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:29.200
until it closes] Barney’s Beanery, you felt
that synergy that maybe the other projects

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:36.350
that were good times but there was something
different because you went by those [tenets]

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:37.350
what you’re saying right now.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:41.290
John: Yes, I let the work guide me and the
material and put my belief system in what

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:53.320
we were doing and the good it could do first,
it wasn’t about being a filmmaker in the

00:09:53.320 --> 00:10:00.440
sense of producing and all of the functional
stuff, everybody did there job and they came

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:11.860
with their tools to do a great job and so
it was more about that emotion and the feeling

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:17.940
of having created something that everybody
was really proud of in themselves not for

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:24.290
just the craft of it was this like…we did
something…we did something…Oh my gosh,

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.500
it affected people…Oh my gosh?

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:32.120
And grown women coming out and crying and
holding on to our actors and saying “I didn’t

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:40.480
know that this existed and that this happened.”
And they don’t know each other, she’s

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:47.019
coming up…there was such a strong reaction.
That is so valuable as a human-to-human-thing,

00:10:47.019 --> 00:10:53.529
to connect with another human being that is
“not your own.” And it spoke to a deeper

00:10:53.529 --> 00:11:01.889
thing about the movie which is THE CHILD’S
VOICE is the calling of the child within us.

00:11:01.889 --> 00:11:09.139
And the answer to the child that is not you
or yours to say I will protect you, I will

00:11:09.139 --> 00:11:18.570
help you, I will risk my life because that
in itself is our mammal part (the human part)

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:21.010
of us that makes us different from a reptile.

